48.6 180.9,251.3 181.3,254.0 181.8,256.7 182.2,259.4 182.6,262.2 183.1,264.9 183.5,267.7 184.0,270.5 184.4,273.3 184.8,276.1 185.3,278.9 185.7,281.8 186.2,284.7 186.6,287.6 187.0,290.5 187.5,293.4 187.9,296.4 188.4,299.3 188.8,302.3 189.2,305.3 189.7,308.3 190.1,311.4 190.6,314.4 191.0,317.5 191.4,320.6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7A0000\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"114.0\" cy=\"48.0\" r=\"3.4\" fill=\"#7A0000\"/><text x=\"120.0\" y=\"52.0\" font-family=\"Public Sans\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#7A0000\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">(2; 9)</text><circle cx=\"48.0\" cy=\"246.0\" r=\"3.4\" fill=\"#7A0000\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"238.0\" font-family=\"Public Sans\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#7A0000\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">−1</text><circle cx=\"180.0\" cy=\"246.0\" r=\"3.4\" fill=\"#7A0000\"/><text x=\"185.0\" y=\"238.0\" font-family=\"Public Sans\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#7A0000\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">5</text><circle cx=\"70.0\" cy=\"136.0\" r=\"3.4\" fill=\"#7A0000\"/></svg></span><div class=\"cap\">y = −x² + 4x + 5: turning point (2; 9), x-intercepts −1 and 5, y-intercept 5.</div></div>","ex":{"q":"Find the turning point and intercepts of y = −x² + 4x + 5.","steps":["x = −<span class=\"fr\"><span class=\"n\">4</span><span class=\"d\">2(−1)</span></span> = 2; y = −4 + 8 + 5 = 9. Turning point (2; 9).","x-intercepts: x² − 4x − 5 = 0, x = 5 or −1.","y-intercept 5."]},"watch":"Keep the sign of b: for x² − 6x + 1, x = −<span class=\"fr\"><span class=\"n\">−6</span><span class=\"d\">2(1)</span></span> = 3.","qs":[{"text":"Find the turning point of y = x² − 4x − 5.","vis":"","parts":[{"t":"box","label":"Turning point x","a":"2","form":"val","o":null},{"t":"box","label":"Turning point y","a":"-9","form":"val","o":null}],"hint":"Use x = −b ÷ (2a), then substitute to find y.","steps":"1. Formula: for y = ax² + bx + c, the axis of symmetry is x = −b ÷ (2a). Put this x into the formula to get the y-value of the turning point.<br>2. a = 1, b = −4: x = 4 ÷ 2 = 2<br>3. y = 2² − 4 × 2 − 5 = 4 − 8 − 5 = −9<br>4. Turning point (2; −9)"},{"text":"Find the turning point of y = 2x² + 4x − 6.","vis":"","parts":[{"t":"box","label":"Turning point x","a":"-1","form":"val","o":null},{"t":"box","label":"Turning point y","a":"-8","form":"val","o":null}],"hint":"Use x = −b ÷ (2a).","steps":"1. Formula: for y = ax² + bx + c, the axis of symmetry is x = −b ÷ (2a). Put this x into the formula to get the y-value of the turning point.<br>2. a = 2, b = 4: x = −4 ÷ 4 = −1<br>3. y = 2(−1)² + 4(−1) − 6 = 2 − 4 − 6 = −8<br>4. Turning point (−1; −8)"},{"text":"Find the x-intercepts of y = x² + 2x − 8.","vis":"","parts":[{"t":"box","label":"Larger x-intercept","a":"2","form":"val","o":null},{"t":"box","label":"Smaller x-intercept","a":"-4","form":"val","o":null}],"hint":"Factorise.","steps":"1. Rule (intercepts): for the y-intercept put x = 0; for the x-intercept put y = 0.<br>2. x² + 2x − 8 = 0<br>3. Rule: write the equation = 0, factorise, then set each factor equal to 0.<br>4. (x + 4)(x − 2) = 0<br>5. x = 2 or x = −4"}]}